\f0\fs22 \cf2 The Elvi held the second inauguration in the same room as the first, for the excellent reason that they only had the one. Parliament House had been a brothel called Clambake for the better part of a century, and the name was still up over the door in gold leaf, and nobody had ever proposed taking it down. There had been a motion once. It had been ruled out of order on the grounds that it was in the wrong key.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs22 \cf2 The difference between the first inauguration and the second was that you could see the floor.\
At the first, the crowd had been a riot of leather and cowboy outfits and sequins packed shoulder to shoulder from the bar to the staircase, and a man could not have got a hand into his own pocket. Now there was carpet showing. There was a good deal of carpet showing, worn through to the weave in two places and stained in more, and the Elvi had never in their lives had cause to look at it, and they were not looking at it now either, because looking at it meant something.\
Walter had a seat on the second step of the dais, which is where a fifteen-year-old goes when the Holy Momma has brought him and nobody wants to say no to the Holy Momma. He was wearing a shirt with a collar. He had a rolled-up festival poster in his fist and he had been holding it the same way for forty minutes.\
Above him, on a throne with gilt trimmings and oceanic velvet and no padding to speak of, Sarah Jacobson sat with her hands on the arms and her feet flat on the floor and thirty-seven weeks of the One After the One out in front of her. The thrones were on their fourth career now. Burlesque, then the Inauguration, then the Domain, and now this. They had been trucked back from the festival on the Tuesday, along with a great deal of scaffolding and about nine hundred unsold Air Guitars.\
The other throne was empty. Everybody had made a point of not looking at that either, and then the Elvi had put the Great Teacher in it forty minutes late, in the gold jumpsuit and the cape and the sash and the crown of part-melted bobbleheads, and the room had gone up like a struck match, and after that it was fine.\
Aunt Kathy stood at the foot of the dais in a tie-dyed lab coat with a canvas bag over her shoulder that had a corner of something rigid poking out of it. Aunt Hayley stood beside her with her back to the wall, and had been counting since she came in.\
At three minutes past the hour a Clambake-series Elvi called Scott Heyward climbed the two wooden steps that had been screwed to the side of the spa bath and stood on the mosaics, where the mermaids had been looking world-weary since before anybody in the room was born.\
\'93My fellow Elvi,\'94 Scott Heyward said. \'93Praise the Dawkins.\'94\
\f1\i Praise the Dawkins! Bless the Holy Momma!\f0\i0 \
It came back thinner than it should have. Scott Heyward waited a moment to see whether it would come back again, the way it always had, and it did not, so he went on.\
\'93The first Elvi Parliament of the Ghetto is in session. There is one item.\'94\
He read it off a card. It had been typed, and then somebody had gone over the typing in biro to make it look official.\
\'93By decree of the Parliament, in the matter of the Supreme Kingness. That the office of the High Elvis is continuous and shall not lapse. That the High Elvis shall be, and shall always be, of the same series as the first. That the character of the office and the character of the man are the one article. That there shall be no difficulty.\'94\
There was applause. It was not the applause of people who had understood a legal instrument. It was the applause of people who had been told there would be no difficulty.\
\'93Brothers and sisters,\'94 said Scott Heyward, \'93the High Elvis.\'94\
He came in from the mezzanine stairs, and he came in at a stroll, in a suit that was doing none of the shouting, and he had the same face.\
He had exactly the same face. That is the whole of it, and it does not improve with elaboration. He had the same shoulders and the same unhurried way of putting one foot down after the other, and when he took the two wooden steps up onto the spa bath he took them the same way, and when he lifted his hand the room came apart.\
Walter did not chant. Walter turned his head to follow the man across the room the way you follow a wasp, and then he sat still with his mouth slightly open, and then he looked down at the poster in his fist and rolled it a little tighter, and then he looked back up.\
Aunt Kathy put a hand on the top of his head, briefly, and took it away again.\
\'93My fellow Elvi,\'94 said the High Elvis. \'93Praise the Dawkins.\'94\
\f1\i Praise the Dawkins! Bless the Holy Momma!\f0\i0 \
Louder this time. Much louder. It had needed the face.\
\'93I am all shook up,\'94 he said, \'93by the honour you bestow upon me.\'94\
The Elvi made a noise. Some of them had been at the first one and knew what was coming and were already halfway to weeping.\
\'93I stand where a better man stood, and I will not pretend to you that I do not feel the draught. But I have a promised land in mind for us, and there is no time for crying in the ghetto. Memories are a fine thing and I will remember him, but a man who lives on memories is a man with a wooden heart. It\'92s now or never, brothers and sisters. The fair is moving on.\'94\
\f1\i High Elvis! Praise the Dawkins!\f0\i0 \
\'93They came into our street with a sickness in a can. They did not have the decency to look us in the eye while they did it. Well. There goes my everything, some of you are saying, and I say to you: not while I am standing on a bath. We have separate ways in front of us and we will not take them. If I can dream, I dream of every one of you following that dream with me, and I dream of us cleaning up our own backyard, because we have done it once already and we know where the hoses are.\'94\
\f1\i Clean the Domain! Clean the Domain!\f0\i0 \
\'93That was the other one,\'94 said the High Elvis. \'93But I\'92ll take it.\'94\
Laughter, and it went on longer than the joke deserved, the way laughter does at a wake.\
\'93Only the strong survive,\'94 he said, and let it sit. \'93That is what they say to us, brothers and sisters. That is what has been said to the Elvi since there were Elvi, and they say it as though it settles the matter. I have been the High Elvis for four minutes and I will tell you what I think of it.\'94 He looked around the room, at all that carpet. \'93I think it depends entirely on what you are prepared to be strong about.\'94\
\f1\i High Elvis! High Elvis!\f0\i0 \
\'93Thank you very much,\'94 said the High Elvis, and stepped down.\
That was the ceremonial part. The Elvi had the beers out before he reached the floor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs22 \cf2 Aunt Kathy got to him at the bar, which had been slouching along that wall with its mahogany worn smooth for a hundred and forty years and had never once been out of stock.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs22 \cf2 \'93I need eleven minutes,\'94 she said.\
\'93Aunt Kathy.\'94 He inclined his head. \'93You may have twelve.\'94\
She put the canvas bag on the bar and took the rigid thing out of it, which turned out to be a tablet the size of a breakfast tray, and she laid it flat and turned it round to face him.\
\'93I\'92ve got it,\'94 she said. \'93The thing that\'92s killing you. I isolated it Tuesday. It\'92s a pararetrovirus, it\'92s been written to go off in Elvi DNA and nothing else, and it does not muck about. Hours. Not days.\'94\
\'93Can you stop it?\'94\
\'93No.\'94\
\'93Ah,\'94 said the High Elvis.\
\'93I can make the other thing,\'94 Kathy said. \'93There\'92s another thing. It\'92s the same platform running the other way and I can grow it in a dish, and at the rate I can grow it in a dish I will have enough to save nine of you by Christmas.\'94 She said it flatly and did not soften it. \'93So that\'92s not it either. That\'92s not the answer, that\'92s a hobby.\'94\
\'93Aunt Kathy,\'94 said the High Elvis, \'93you have not come here to tell me that.\'94\
\'93No,\'94 she said. \'93I\'92ve come because of the room.\'94\
Aunt Hayley had come off the wall.\
\'93Forty-one,\'94 Hayley said.\
\'93Forty-one what?\'94 said the High Elvis.\
\'93Forty-one Elvi in this room. I\'92ve counted three times, and I\'92ve counted the ones in the kitchen.\'94 She had a notebook, an actual paper one, because she had been a Detective Superintendent before she was anything else and the habit had outlived the employer. \'93Nineteen of them were on the plaza on Thursday. I asked.\'94\
\'93Nineteen.\'94 The High Elvis put his beer down. \'93That is not the proportion I would have expected.\'94\
\'93It is not the proportion anyone would have expected. Eleven of the nineteen are Elvines.\'94 Hayley turned the notebook round, which was pointless, since nobody could read her handwriting. \'93They were on the plaza. They breathed what everyone else breathed. And they\'92re standing over there arguing about a car.\'94\
They were. Dianne Carter and Maile Duval and Rusty Martin and eight others, in a knot by the moth-eaten curtains, and the argument was about whether a Lincoln could be considered a Cadillac if you were desperate, and every one of them had skin like the inside of a shell.\
\'93They look well,\'94 said the High Elvis.\
\'93They look \f1\i ridiculous\f0\i0 ,\'93 said Kathy. \'94Nobody looks like that. I\'92ve been a doctor for a long time and I have never once walked into a room and thought, hello, everyone here is at the absolute top of their form. It doesn\'92t happen. It didn\'92t happen at a wedding. It\'92s happening in a brothel eight days after a massacre.\'93\
Walter had come down off the step to get closer to the beer he was not going to be given, which put him at Kathy\'92s elbow, which put him within a metre and a half of the Great Teacher when the Great Teacher arrived to see what everyone was looking at.\
Chanta had four Elvisettes with him. He had been going nowhere without four Elvisettes for some weeks and it had stopped being remarked on. Betty Lee was on his left with her hair gone entirely back to blonde at the roots, and Marguerita was on his right, and the other two were behind, and all four of them had the look as well, only more so.\
\'93Great Teacher,\'94 said the High Elvis.\
\'93Yeah, gidday.\'94 Chanta had a beer in each hand and had not worked out how to shake with either. \'93Good speech. Bit sad in the middle.\'94\
\'93It was intended to be.\'94\
\'93Nailed it, then.\'94\
Kathy looked at Betty Lee for slightly too long.\
\'93What?\'94 said Betty Lee.\
\'93How old are you?\'94\
\'93Rude,\'94 said Betty Lee.\
\'93She\'92s terrific, isn\'92t she,\'94 said Chanta, pleased. \'93They\'92re all terrific. It\'92s the clean living. I\'92ve been saying, haven\'92t I been saying? Off the smokes, plenty of fresh air. Marguerita had a crook knee for six years and now she\'92s doing the splits at parties.\'94 He raised one of the beers in tribute. \'93Whole crew\'92s blooming. Honest to tax, they walk in ordinary and about a fortnight later they\'92re all glowing away like they\'92ve swallowed a lamp. I put it down to the catering.\'94\
\'93Fortnight,\'94 said Kathy.\
\'93About a fortnight. Give or take.\'94\
Kathy looked at Hayley. Hayley had stopped writing.\
\'93Chanta,\'94 said Kathy. \'93The Elvines over there by the curtains.\'94\
\'93Dianne and that lot.\'94 Chanta brightened. \'93Yeah, they\'92re lovely.\'94\
\'93How lovely?\'94\
\'93Very lovely.\'94\
\'93Chanta.\'94\
\'93They\'92re \f1\i very\f0\i0  lovely,\'93 said Chanta, with the emphasis of a man being asked to repeat himself for no reason he could see, and then, because he was constitutionally unable to leave a silence alone, and because a corner of him had spent his whole life waiting for somebody in a position of authority to ask him a question he could answer, he filled it. \'94All of them, if you want the truth. Every one of them. I\'92m not proud of it. Well. I\'92m a bit proud of it.\'93\
Nobody said anything.\
It took about two seconds, which in a room of that size is a considerable length of time. Hayley got there first, because it was her job. The High Elvis got there second and did not move a muscle. Kathy got there third and put both hands flat on the bar. Sarah Jacobson, twelve feet away on a throne with no padding, got there somewhere in among that and turned her head very slowly towards the curtains.\
Walter got there fourth, and Walter was fifteen, and Walter was already grinning like a split melon before anyone had said a word.\
Chanta looked around at the faces.\
\'93What,\'94 he said.\
Then, a moment later: \'93What?\'94\
Then he counted something on his fingers, twice, and stopped.\
\'93Oh,\'94 said Chanta.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs22 \cf2 \'93Say it out loud,\'94 said Kathy. \'93Somebody say it out loud, because I\'92ve been carrying it round in a bag since Tuesday and I\'92d like to hear how stupid it sounds.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs22 \cf2 \'93He\'92s carrying the other thing,\'94 said Hayley. \'93The one you can grow nine of by Christmas.\'94\
\'93He\'92s carrying it, he\'92s been carrying it for months, and he\'92s been distributing it like a man handing out flyers.\'94 Kathy did not look at Chanta while she said it, which was as close to mercy as she got. \'93Eleven Elvines walked into a gas attack designed to kill Elvines and walked out of it. And the only thing eleven Elvines have in common in this entire taxing city is standing there in a cape holding two beers.\'94\
\'93Three, now,\'94 said Chanta, who had acquired another one.\
From the throne, Sarah said: \'93Then it can be moved.\'94\
She said it the way a person says a thing they have just this second thought of. She had put no particular weight on it. She was looking at the curtains and not at anybody.\
\'93Ms Jacobson?\'94 said the High Elvis.\
\'93If it got from him to them, it isn\'92t stuck in him. That\'92s all. Things that move once move again.\'94 She shifted her weight in the throne, which took a while, and which she conducted with the patience of a woman lowering a piano. \'93You don\'92t need a laboratory to spread something that\'92s already spreading. You need to stop treating it like an accident.\'94\
\'93Holy Momma \'97\'94\
\'93Sarah.\'94\
\'93Sarah,\'94 said the High Elvis, \'93you are describing a public health policy.\'94\
\'93I\'92m describing what\'92s already happened. You can call it what you like.\'94\
Kathy had gone very still, and then she started to laugh, and it was not a nice laugh, and she put her hand over her eyes.\
\'93Oh, no,\'94 she said. \'93No, no, no.\'94\
\'93What,\'94 said Hayley.\
\'93I\'92m the doctor. I\'92m going to have to be the one who says it.\'94 Kathy took her hand down. \'93Right. Medical opinion. Everybody has to root everybody.\'94\
There was a pause of the kind that gets described in Parliamentary records as \f1\i the House being briefly unable to proceed\f0\i0 .\
\'93That\'92s the prescription,\'94 said Kathy. \'93That\'92s it. That\'92s the whole of it. It goes body to body, it goes fast, it\'92s already got a head start of about eleven Elvines and however many of Chanta\'92s ladies, and there are what, ninety-odd thousand of you left, and the thing that\'92s killing you works in \f1\i hours\f0\i0 . You can\'92t outrun it in a dish. You can outrun it in a bed.\'93 She spread her hands. \'94I\'92d like the record to show I was joking for the first four seconds.\'93\
\'93You were not joking,\'94 said Hayley.\
\'93I was joking for four seconds.\'94\
Scott Heyward had arrived with the card still in his hand and an expression of profound administrative distress. \'93There is the Oath,\'94 he said.\
\'93What about the Oath?\'94\
\'93The Oath says \f1\i no excess after midnight\f0\i0 .\'93\
\'93Then do it before midnight,\'94 said Kathy.\
\'93That is a very glib answer to a serious question of the covenant,\'94 said Scott Heyward, and would have gone on, except that Dianne Carter had come over from the curtains with the other ten behind her, and Dianne Carter had been on the plaza on Thursday, and had held a Blue Hawaii-series drummer while he stopped, and had walked home through a cordon on her own two legs feeling absolutely marvellous, and had not been able to look at herself in a mirror since.\
\'93I\'92ll do it,\'94 said Dianne Carter.\
\'93Sister \'97\'94\
\'93I\'92ll do it, and I\'92ll do it in the street, and I\'92ll do it in front of the Health Department.\'94 She was not shouting. It was worse than shouting. \'93Do you know what I\'92ve been doing for eight days, Scott? I\'92ve been going to funerals in perfect health. I\'92ve been the healthiest thing at four funerals. Cousins of mine are in the ground and I have never felt better in my life and nobody would tell me why.\'94 She turned on Kathy. \'93Is that it? Is that why?\'94\
\'93Almost certainly,\'94 said Kathy.\
\'93Then I want it on the noticeboard by tonight.\'94\
\'93Sister, there are \f1\i proprieties\f0\i0  \'97\'93\
\'93There are \f1\i forty-one of us in this room\f0\i0 ,\'93 said Dianne Carter.\
That did it. It went round the floor like a dropped tray. Maile Duval wanted to know whether it worked in both directions, which was a better question than anyone gave her credit for. Rusty Martin wanted a roster. A Tulsa at the back asked whether it counted if you were married, and three separate Elvi answered him at once and none of the answers agreed. Somebody wanted to know if there was paperwork. Somebody else said there had better not be. Two Elvi who had spent the whole festival on opposite sides of the lyric question found themselves in furious agreement for the first time in their lives and neither of them noticed.\
Kathy, in the middle of it, reached under her lab coat and put a fresh patch on her belly with the practised flick of a woman who has done it in worse rooms than this. Hayley, still watching the argument, still not writing anything, moved in against her side and slid her hand under the coat and peeled it off again, unhurried, the way you take a fork off a toddler, and put it in her own pocket. Kathy leaned her head on Hayley\'92s shoulder and kept arguing.\
Walter watched the entire manoeuvre from a distance of one metre and filed it away for the rest of his life.\
The High Elvis had said nothing for some minutes. He picked up his beer, looked at it, and put it down without drinking any.\
\'93Great Teacher,\'94 he said. \'93You understand what is being asked.\'94\
\'93Sort of,\'94 said Chanta.\
\'93You understand that you did not do this on purpose.\'94\
\'93I definitely didn\'92t do anything on purpose,\'94 said Chanta, with feeling.\
\'93No,\'94 said the High Elvis. \'93You never do. It is the most reliable thing about you.\'94 He stepped away from the bar and went back to the spa bath, and the room noticed him going, and by the time he had his foot on the second wooden step it had stopped arguing on its own.\
He did not do the ceremonial voice. He did the other one, the small close one, a man talking over a fence at dusk.\
\'93Brothers and sisters. My first decree.\'94\
They waited. At the first inauguration the first decree had been a clean-up, and the room had gone silent, and a lone voice at the back had said \f1\i we\'92re musicians, not street sweepers\f0\i0 , and the man on this bath had carried that silence around with him for a year.\
\'93There is a medicine,\'94 said the High Elvis, \'93and the Great Teacher is standing in it. It does not come in a bottle. It comes the way the Dawkins arranges most things, which is to say badly, and cheaply, and without asking anybody\'92s permission, and it works.\'94 He let that go round once. \'93So. Go and get it. Go and give it. Take it to your neighbours, take it to your cousins, take it to that fellow in the Pale you have been circling for two years and never had a reason. You have a reason. I am giving you the reason. It is now or never, and I want a big hunk o\'92 love in every street in this Ghetto by the weekend.\'94\
The Clambake came off its hinges.\
They were still cheering when he added, \'93And do the clam in the Clambake, if you must, but not on the carpet, it is a heritage item,\'94 which almost nobody heard, and which he said anyway.\
He came down off the bath into the crush and they took him along with them, and he went. He was six paces from the doors when he stopped, turned round, came back, and held out his hand to the boy on the second step of the dais.\
Walter gave him the poster.\
The High Elvis unrolled it against the balustrade. It was the Rock Against Revival bill, a fortnight old, four thousand names down the back of it in six-point type, and one of them was his.\
He signed it across the top, and he handed it back, and he said, \'93Thank you very much,\'94 and then he was gone with the rest of them out into the Ghetto.\
Walter stood there and looked at the signature for a while.\
It was the same one.\
